# Etiuda - public repo
#
# This is an ALLOWLIST, not the usual blocklist: everything is ignored, and the few files the
# repo should contain are named back in. The inversion is deliberate.
#
# Etiuda is an engine that ships with no content of its own. The content lives in catalog
# files, which are normally confidential - customer wording, internal policy, whatever the
# deployment is about - and they sit in this folder so the engine can load them. With a
# blocklist, committing one is the DEFAULT and safety rests on having predicted every filename,
# including files nobody has created yet. With an allowlist a new file is ignored until someone
# deliberately adds it, so the failure mode is "forgot to publish something" rather than
# "published a customer's macros". Only one of those can be undone.
#
# To publish a file, name it below. `git status --ignored` shows what is being held back.

# Ignore everything
/*

# ...except the engine and its paperwork
!/.gitignore
!/Etiuda.html
!/README.md
!/LICENSE

# Local tooling, deliberately unpublished. The allowlist above already ignores these; they are
# named explicitly so the intent is on the record and nobody "helpfully" allowlists them later.
# test.js is our own harness (unit tests + catalog linter) and build-integrated.js carries
# absolute paths from a personal machine - neither is part of what this repo publishes.
/test.js
/build-integrated.js

# So: no catalog is ever committed - not etiuda-catalog.js, not an export under any other
# name - and neither is anything else that lands here.
#
# .gitignore does not stop `git add -f`. Keep this folder to the engine and its catalog;
# working notes and anything personal belong outside the repo entirely.
